SweepRx - initial TCP receive tuning sweep

Run SweepRx on the Amiga, from a LOCAL Shell. It needs a running AmiNetXDuo
with receive tuning support and an online, up interface with an IPv4 address.
Close active TCP clients, including remote Shell, browser, SMB and FTP sessions.
Do not change interface settings or run another benchmark during the sweep.
Other interfaces share the packet pool, so keep their traffic quiet too.

Copy rx-sweep-peer.py to a second machine on the same LAN. It needs Python 3
and no extra packages. Replace 192.168.1.50 with the chosen Amiga interface's
address. Start the sender there:

  python3 rx-sweep-peer.py 192.168.1.50

Then on the Amiga (replace eth0 with your interface name):

  SweepRx eth0 DRYRUN
  SweepRx eth0 TO=RAM:eth0-sweep.csv

The sender retries while the interface is offline between trials. Stop it with
Ctrl-C when SweepRx has finished; it also stops after 1800 seconds by default.
Use --duration 3600 for a longer sweep, and matching --port / PORT options if
5001 is occupied. The peer sends only to the specified address and port.
Keep the peer/path faster than the Amiga; a limited sender masks improvements.
Its sent-byte counts include queued data: the Amiga's receive counts are the
measurement. This sender is for TCP receive trials, not an iperf3 server.

Defaults and bounds

The default grid is IPREQUESTS=0,8,16 crossed with TCPWINDOW=0,16384,65536.
All other knobs keep their saved requested values. Every candidate is run
three times for ten seconds, with three original-settings baseline trials
before and after. An exact duplicate of the original settings is skipped.
The default sweep takes roughly five to seven minutes on a responsive LAN.
Trials use fresh TCP connections and the same blocking 64 KB receive path as
iperf. Timing starts with the first received bytes and includes TCP startup;
there is no excluded warmup interval in this first version.

Every tuning knob accepts a comma-separated list (quote it if your Shell
requires that). Zero means automatic, including when restoring settings.

  IPREQUESTS, ARPREQUESTS       0..128
  TCPWINDOW                    0..16777216 bytes
  TCPACKMAX, TCPGROWRTT         0..65535
  RXRUNMAX, RXREPOST            0..128

At most eight distinct values per list and 64 combinations before removal of
an original-settings duplicate. REPEATS must be odd, 1..9 (default 3).
TIME is 1..120 seconds (default 10). WAIT is 1..300 seconds (default 30):
the total trial deadline is WAIT + TIME + five seconds of allowance.
PORT is 1..65535 (default 5001). DRYRUN prints the plan, writes no files and
changes no settings; TO is required for a real sweep. Existing results and
recovery files are never deliberately overwritten; use a new TO path.

Refine the best depth/window combination in a smaller second sweep, e.g.:

  SweepRx eth0 TO=RAM:eth0-refine.csv IPREQUESTS=16 TCPWINDOW=16384 RXREPOST=1,2,4 RXRUNMAX=16,64,128

Those numbers illustrate the syntax; they are not recommendations for a card.
You can then vary TCPACKMAX and TCPGROWRTT with depth/window held fixed.

Results and recovery

SweepRx writes a CSV file and a sibling .restore AmigaDOS script BEFORE its
first interface change. Use a local disk path instead of RAM: to retain them
across reboot. Each trial takes the interface offline, applies all seven
settings, brings it online, waits one second and checks requested readback.
The listener binds the selected interface's IPv4 address, not the wildcard.
The interface must keep that address throughout the sweep.

Original requested values are restored and the interface brought online on
completion, Ctrl-C, missing peer, write error or other handled failure. A
restoration failure is reported prominently and the command returns failure.
After a crash or failed restoration, close network clients and run locally:

  Execute RAM:eth0-sweep.csv.restore

This restores the saved temporary values, which may differ from the config
file's values. It does not use RESETTUNING or rewrite any configuration file.
No candidate is automatically kept or made permanent. A second SweepRx is
refused while one runs. After a process crash, reboot before another sweep if
the command still reports an existing sweep.

The CSV begins with a # metadata line; skip comment lines in CSV readers.
A final # status marker distinguishes a complete sweep from an aborted run;
a missing marker means the file may be incomplete.
phase 0 is the opening baseline, 1 a candidate, 2 the closing baseline.
case is a zero-based plan index for phase 1. Requested and effective columns
are separate: the pool may clamp read depth; automatic policies resolve at
runtime. Hardware bytes, packet pool counts and batch mode are included.
Effective values and pool availability are sampled before each measurement.
bytes_hi/bytes_lo are the two halves of the 64-bit byte count.

valid=0 means a short/zero-rate trial or a rise in receive errors, overruns or
packet allocation failures (or interface identity/address change). All repeats
must be valid to rank a candidate. Medians and each raw trial are printed;
redirect console output to retain the summary as well. An aborted sweep keeps
its completed rows, returns failure, and makes no recommendation. Receive
error counters do not include all possible network loss: TCP retransmissions,
CPU load and latency are not measured in this version. A high throughput
result alone is not evidence of correct payload delivery or good latency.

The final report names a best measured candidate only when it beats both valid
baseline medians and those medians differ by no more than about ten percent.
The candidate order rotates between repetitions to spread gradual drift.
Treat a small gain or wide variation as noise: repeat the sweep, then test
normal applications. The tool does not claim statistical significance.

TCPWINDOW caps established connections; the initial SYN/SYN-ACK window remains
automatic. Its effective column is the configured cap, not the per-socket
advertisement after pool/MSS/RTT/scaling constraints. RXBUFFER describes the
card's actual capacity and is not swept. Device batch reads keep their own
repost policy; RXREPOST may consequently have little effect in batch mode.
This first version supports one interface and IPv4 TCP receive at a time.

Transmit controls

ConfigureNetInterface INTERFACE TXTUNING reports independent transmit knobs:
WRITEREQUESTS (outstanding writes), TXRUNMAX (frames per transmit run), and
TXREAP (completion collection policy). They can be changed temporarily while
the interface is offline and fully drained, with no active TCP clients.
RESETTXTUNING restores their attachment-time values. See the user guide for
ranges, requested/effective readback and hardware/build fallbacks. SweepRx's
current grid still measures receive and changes only the seven receive knobs.
